-For robots, these biological architectures can be implemented as synthetic nervous systems (SNSs): networks of conductance-based spiking and non-spiking neurons that are small enough to run in real time yet biologically grounded enough to be compared against animal data. The approach has been pursued most persistently by the insect-biorobotics lineage of the Quinn laboratory at Case Western Reserve University and by laboratories closely connected to it, the author's own included. Works in this lineage span several platforms: CPG-based neural control of a pneumatically actuated dog robot \citep{hunt_development_2017}, analytically designed dynamic neural networks for human balance control \citep{hilts_dynamic_2019}, the full-muscle rat hindlimb model and its moment-arm analysis \citep{young_analyzing_2019}, the two-layer CPG neuromechanical walker built on it \citep{deng_neuromechanical_2019}, which supplies this dissertation's controller architecture, and the Muscle Mutt quadruped, a canine-inspired BPA robot designed at the author's laboratory, the Agile and Adaptive Robotics Laboratory (AARL) at Portland State University, for synthetic nervous system control \citep{scharzenberger_design_2019}. Related controllers from the wider neuromechanical community include sensory-entrained CPG control of bipedal walking \citep{li_bipedal_2017}, inter-leg coordination analyses in stick-insect-inspired controllers \citep{nourse_analyzing_2019}, reinforcement-reflex hybrids in insects \citep{goldsmith_investigating_2021}, and adaptive hindlimb walking controllers \citep{schilling_adaptive_2022}.
+For robots, these biological architectures can be implemented as synthetic nervous systems (SNSs): networks of conductance-based spiking and non-spiking neurons that are small enough to run in real time yet biologically grounded enough to be compared against animal data. The approach has been pursued most persistently by the insect-biorobotics lineage of the Quinn laboratory at Case Western Reserve University and by laboratories closely connected to it, my own included. Works in this lineage span several platforms: CPG-based neural control of a pneumatically actuated dog robot \citep{hunt_development_2017}, analytically designed dynamic neural networks for human balance control \citep{hilts_dynamic_2019}, the full-muscle rat hindlimb model and its moment-arm analysis \citep{young_analyzing_2019}, the two-layer CPG neuromechanical walker built on it \citep{deng_neuromechanical_2019}, which supplies this dissertation's controller architecture, and the Muscle Mutt quadruped, a canine-inspired BPA robot designed at my laboratory, the Agile and Adaptive Robotics Laboratory (AARL) at Portland State University, for synthetic nervous system control \citep{scharzenberger_design_2019}. Related controllers from the wider neuromechanical community include sensory-entrained CPG control of bipedal walking \citep{li_bipedal_2017}, inter-leg coordination analyses in stick-insect-inspired controllers \citep{nourse_analyzing_2019}, reinforcement-reflex hybrids in insects \citep{goldsmith_investigating_2021}, and adaptive hindlimb walking controllers \citep{schilling_adaptive_2022}.
